      "student_id": "profile-ca7c8cf3-33a8-4e47-9fb8-22b552bfaf1f",
      "prompt": "I would hide the storage representation inside the module and preserve the public operations and their behavior. Clients should not need to change when I replace the representation.",
      "response": "You've got the right principle — \"clients should not need to change when I replace the representation\" is exactly the goal. But your answer stays at the principle level; the question wanted it operationalized. Here's what each part should look like:\n\n**Part 1 — which module(s) change?** Exactly one: `StudentRecords`. The procedure is called *change impact analysis*: pick a plausible change (SQL instead of CSV), trace what would have be edited, and count the modules that light up. A well-hidden design lights up **one** module; if callers light up too, the secret is leaking.\n\n**Part 2 — what's free vs. frozen.** Free to change (all inside the module): the storage mechanism (CSV reader → DB connection/driver), the internal record representation (row parsing → ORM objects), the file/table layout, internal helpers for opening or migrating storage. Not allowed to change: the three public names `find`, `gpa`, `add`; their parameter and return types (`str`, `StudentProfile`, `float`, `None`); and the behavior clients rely on. The interface is \"permission to assume\" — once published, every name and type is a fact clients may build on.\n\n**Part 3 — proving it without reading the source.** Run an **implementation-swap test**: keep the client code identical, wire in the SQL-backed implementation instead of the CSV one, and check whether the clients still compile and pass their tests unchanged. Same client code, two implementations. If clients survive the swap untouched, the storage secret is genuinely hidden. (This is also why `private` alone proves nothing: fields can be private while a return type like `sqlite3.Row` still hands the storage decision to every caller.)\n\nQuick check: if `find` were declared `-> sqlite3.Row` instead of `-> StudentProfile`, would your implementation-swap test still pass without touching client code? Why or why not?",
